1) indentation

Indentation is made with tabs, and tabs are 8 characters, period. Besides all perfectly valid
reasons provided by [1], I'll add that if you, for whatever the reason, find yourself editing
code with a standard text editor, for example nano, indentation with spaces is nothing less than
torture. That is, the GNU standard [2] is NOT accepted. The only exception to the "never use
spaces for indentation" rule is when splitting long lines and the last tab is too long in order
to align properly, for example with long function parameter lists, see 0).

2) line length and breaks

My preference for line length has increased from the traditional 80 characters to 100, simply
because standard screen real estate has become larger. Moreover, it better allows end-of-line
comments which I often consider helpful and appropriate. My standard terminal window is set to
open up with 105 columns in order to fit a line numbered nano session covering 100 characters.

3) braces

Opening braces for blocks are placed last on the line and for function definitions at the
beginning of next line. Closing brace alone on the line, except for continuing statements (i.e.
else, while, etc.). Do not use braces for single statements. Always put single statements on
a separate line, really simple 'one-liners', see 0), may be kept as one line, unless it clutters
the code too much (programmer's freedom with responsibility). Examples are single case branches
and simple if (cond) do_this; However, enclose multilevel single statement in braces. The GNU
standard [2] with 2 spaces extra indentation and the opening brace on a separate line is in my
considered view not only very hard to read (no alignment between block beginning and end, it also
makes the code appear thoroughly scattered) and extremely ugly (and certainly not even close to
be compatible with the 8 character and tab indentation requirement). For me this GNU brace
placement style is, frankly speaking, simply daft.

4) spaces

DO put a space after keywords, NOT after function names, to indicate the difference, you see!
However, the Linux style exceptions for keywords such as sizeof, typeof, etc. [1] is valid and
should be used (because these keywords are used in the same way as functions). That is,
once again, the GNU standard [2] with its excessive use of spaces is NOT accepted.

Do NOT add spaces inside parenthesis, but DO put one after each comma in parameter lists.
Also do NOT add spaces around structure member operators. DO put one space around binary and
ternary operators, but NOT after unary and prefix increment and decrement operators, also NOT
before postfix operators.

Do NOT leave trailing whitespace.

5) naming

Follow the Linux style [1]. That is, local variables within functions - which should be short and
self-explanatory to the greatest extent possible - should be short and symbolic, preferably
single or only few characters long. Global variables (to be avoided) and global function names
(i.e. declared in header files) should be descriptive (but to the point, i.e. as short as
possible). Do NOT use mixed-cased names, uppercase letters are "reserved" for constants, macros,
and suchlike. Use underscores to separate words. CamelCase is a definitive no go.

For local scope function names, naming is essentially free, making personal shortcuts acceptable.
Therefore, always declare them static to the translation unit. However, as it is a quite common
scenario that functions which initially are local to a translation unit later become global
(i.e. are added to the header file and used in another unit), the recommendation is to always
use the global function naming style.

6) typedefs

Here my preference differs slightly from the Linux style in that I tend to find typedefs for
structs (not for pointers) somewhat "stylish". Structs are the "objects", the "custom" types
of the C language, and typedefs give them names. Moreover, I like the fact that editors tend
to provide syntax highlightning for typedefs (at least if they have the format: my_struct_t),
making a typedef'ed struct appear as a type, which it in some sense is. This is also why
typedef:ing pointers is bad, it makes a pointer to an object appear being an object.

However, I agree with that the primary benefit lies where it is explicitly intended to hide the
inner structure of structs (and use accessor functions). The main difference in preference
is -where- I consider such "hiding" is appropriate. For me, program/library specific structures,
only to be used with that specific library are perfectly valid to be typedef:ed "objects". But
then, they should be declared and explained in the main header file that must be included in order
to use the library. The rationale is that as a programmer you should understand how to use
a library before using it. Therefore, it is perfectly fine to have typedefs specific for a lib.

